@{
    Layout = "_Layout.cshtml";
    var title = "About";
}

<article class="page">
    <header class="page-header">
        <h1>About this demo</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="page-body">
        <p class="lede">
            This is a demo site built by <a href="https://github.com/winsonet/statiq_markdown_editor">Statiq Markdown Editor</a>.
            The editor turns any folder of markdown files into a full Statiq static site — see
            <a href="https://github.com/winsonet/statiq_markdown_editor">the README</a> for the full guide.
        </p>

        <h2>What is Statiq?</h2>
        <p>
            <a href="https://www.statiq.dev/">Statiq</a> is a .NET static site generator.
            You write markdown files, drop them in an input folder, and Statiq renders them into HTML
            using Razor templates. It's the same approach Jekyll / Hugo / 11ty take, just in C#.
        </p>

        <h2>What does this editor add?</h2>
        <p>
            A web UI that wraps Statiq with the tools a writer actually wants:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>List view</strong> — every post with category filter and title search.</li>
            <li><strong>Editor view</strong> — Monaco (the engine behind VS Code) with markdown highlighting, paste-to-markdown, paste-to-image.</li>
            <li><strong>New post form</strong> — every frontmatter field rendered as a form, with a live YAML preview alongside.</li>
            <li><strong>Settings</strong> — auto-discovers sites under <code>sites/</code> from each one's <code>config.json</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Four toolbar buttons</strong>:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Build</strong> — runs the in-process Statiq pipeline against the active site (~10 s for a 30-post site).</li>
                    <li><strong>Preview</strong> — builds then starts a local HTTP server on the site's configured port. Auto-kills any stale process on that port.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deploy</strong> — runs the site's per-site <code>build_deploy.sh</code>, which usually rsyncs the output to a VPS and commits the source to GitHub.</li>
                    <li><strong>Git Sync</strong> — runs <code>git_sync.sh</code> standalone, so you can commit &amp; push source-only changes (no rebuild).</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Open the source</h2>
        <p>
            The demo posts and theme live under <code>sites/_demo/</code> and <code>themes/_demo/</code>.
            Clone the repo, <code>dotnet run</code>, click <strong>Build</strong>, then
            <strong>Preview</strong> — you'll be looking at this same site, served from
            <code>http://127.0.0.1:5080</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Want to add a second site? <code>cp -R sites/_demo sites/blog</code>, edit
            <code>sites/blog/config.json</code>, change the theme to your own, point
            <code>Settings → Activate</code> at it, and you're publishing from a fresh site
            without touching the demo's content.
        </p>
    </div>
</article>